# Document validation tool: intended production egress policy

The local subprocess is only a **local process simulation** of the document
boundary. It is not an Azure isolation boundary. The document validation tool
must run in its own Azure Container Apps custom-container session pool,
separate from the Copilot SDK agent pool.

The document pool must:

1. Default-deny all outbound traffic. Document sanitization needs no network.
2. Never receive GitHub, Copilot, AI Gateway, or model credentials.
3. Keep managed identity access disabled inside the session
   (`managedIdentitySettings.lifecycle: None`).
4. Explicitly deny private address ranges, Azure metadata endpoints, and every
   public destination, including `__EGRESS_ALLOW_DOMAIN__`. If a future
   document processor genuinely needs that exact domain, review the threat
   model before allowing HTTPS (TCP 443); never use a wildcard.
5. Disable payload capture. Emit only lifecycle events, duration, verdict code,
   and exit status. Never log document content or sanitized text.
6. Stop each session after the verdict is read; retain a short platform
   cooldown only as a failure fallback.

Before a demo, verify from inside the document session that an HTTPS call to
`__EGRESS_ALLOW_DOMAIN__` and to an unrelated endpoint both fail. Treat either
successful request as a release blocker.
